    "result_text": "Confirmed. `Cp28Tests` fails because the existing `identityKey` logic folds `householdId` (derived from last name + address) into the key \u2014 so two owners with the same telephone but different names/addresses produce different keys and aren't treated as duplicates. That failure is entirely within pre-existing identity-collision code and has no overlap with registration dates. `Cp28`'s payload supplies no `registrationDate`, so my new check (guarded by `supplied != null`) never runs for it \u2014 my change cannot have caused or affected it.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the **no-future-date** rule: a supplied `registrationDate` later than the server date is now rejected with **400 Bad Request**.\n\nChanges:\n\n1. **`FutureRegistrationDateException.java`** (new) \u2014 a `RuntimeException` carrying the rejected `LocalDate`, mirroring the existing `InvalidPostcodeException`/`InvalidEmailException` pattern.\n\n2. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** \u2014 added `rejectFutureRegistrationDate(...)`, wired into `addOwner` right after `rejectCityAtCapacity` and before `defaultRegistrationDate`. It checks the *supplied* value before any defaulting/business-day roll; a `null` date is accepted (it still defaults to the server date), and only a date strictly `isAfter(LocalDate.now())` is rejected.\n\n3. **`ExceptionControllerAdvice.java`** \u2014 added an `@ExceptionHandler` returning 400 with an `errors: [\"registrationDate\"]` property, consistent with the other 400 handlers.\n\nTest results:\n- **`Cp33Tests` (the new no-future-date test): passes.**\n- All registration-date-related and other previously-green tests pass (45 of 46).\n- **One failure, `Cp28Tests`, is pre-existing and unrelated** to this task \u2014 it fails on the identity-key collision rule (expects a same-telephone create to 409, but the current `identityKey` derivation keys on household too). It supplies no `registrationDate`, so my change has no effect on it. I did not modify identity-collision logic, as it's outside the scope of this change. Let me know if you'd like me to look into that separately.",
